{"title":"Circumradius","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>circumradius</b> of a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cyclic\" title=\"Cyclic\">cyclic</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polygon\" title=\"Polygon\">polygon</a> is the radius of the circumscribed circle of that polygon. For a triangle, it is the measure of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Radius\" title=\"Radius\">radius</a> of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circle\" title=\"Circle\">circle</a> that <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumscribe\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Circumscribe\">circumscribes</a> the triangle. Since every triangle is <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Cyclic\" title=\"Cyclic\">cyclic</a>, every triangle has a circumscribed circle, or a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumcircle\" title=\"Circumcircle\">circumcircle</a>.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Formula_for_a_Triangle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Formula for a Triangle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Proof_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Proof_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Formula_for_Circumradius\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Formula for Circumradius</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Circumradius,_bisector_and_altitude\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Circumradius, bisector and altitude</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Euler&#39;s_Theorem_for_a_Triangle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Euler's Theorem for a Triangle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Right_triangles\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Right triangles</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Theorem</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Equilateral_triangles\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Equilateral triangles</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#If_all_three_sides_are_known\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">If all three sides are known</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#If_you_know_just_one_side_and_its_opposite_angle\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">If you know just one side and its opposite angle</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Formula_for_a_Triangle\">Formula for a Triangle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Formula for a Triangle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/3/3834f0cef2a1f4d8524aa5bc8e3faca298550862.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> denote the triangle's three sides and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/7/0a737670d481129e2ead6d7223437f3cf70ea53f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> denote the area of the triangle. Then, the measure of the circumradius of the triangle is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/8/de807d744b4b2313970e68e59371849604fc1855.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=\\frac{abc}{4\\Delta}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"39\" />. This can be rewritten as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/a/47acf06a6c3b174fe323b7c2fc6a3ce148c28056.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta=\\frac{abc}{4R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"70\" height=\"39\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/d/67d2a8dbd287bb04740ee4ebe120cb5274b10d7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4R\\Delta = abc$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"90\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_1\">Proof 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Proof 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/d/3ad58f915ab8e62f7ffd12a4d01aa92205a280f7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pair O, A, B, C, D; O=(0,0); A=(-5,1); B=(1,5); C=(5,1); dot(O); dot (A); dot (B); dot (C); draw(circle(O, sqrt(26))); draw(A--B--C--cycle); D=-B; dot (D); draw(B--D--A); label(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, W); label(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, N); label(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, E); label(&quot;$D$&quot;, D, S); label(&quot;$O$&quot;, O, W); pair E; E=foot(B,A,C); draw(B--E); dot(E); label(&quot;$E$&quot;, E, S); draw(rightanglemark(B,A,D,20)); draw(rightanglemark(B,E,C,20)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"248\" />\n</p><p><br />\nWe let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/4/474cd45035e0eea00cfc2d9355528e69dc34389d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB=c$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/3/da35a0d0a8952651e752f9a0afa0662c0c143b9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC=a$\" width=\"62\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/0/be0834a58059a61a79d86701cc1292ee73a0858c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC=b$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/8/a78a87caa6ddd7c1dde2c59607f3cffbbd52948c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BE=h$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"66\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/7/62712fc4161ade5a16d67406336ededd6dda05c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BO=R$\" width=\"67\" height=\"12\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/5/cb55dc9c50a80e1d9eb23fe53e45f464c0ac755e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle BAD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"59\" height=\"14\" /> is a right angle because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/f/89fff82bb65d0215e49c8c91cb7c553da52205e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"14\" /> is the diameter. Also, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/2/8a2344f80e42487b65b6b0f16f599c3b0ad99d02.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ADB = \\angle BCA$\" width=\"136\" height=\"13\" /> because they both subtend arc <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/7/227ab72958f56d4c78d132fbe1aedc6caec80de4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BAD \\sim \\triangle BEC$\" width=\"143\" height=\"13\" /> by AA similarity, so we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/e/ceef2bbb1ea4d0e9e833a5e37cf786c21b9d465f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{BD}{BA} = \\frac{BC}{BE},\\]\" width=\"93\" height=\"37\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/9/de9737a8c1e3e51286f1e0f3ff93978cb92e268f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2R}{c} = \\frac{a}{h}.\\]\" width=\"67\" height=\"37\" />\nHowever, remember that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/e/37e5e5316f569751ecc7c2c9ce21eff42fc85d0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta = [ABC] = \\frac{bh}{2}\\implies h=\\frac{2\\Delta}{b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"253\" height=\"39\" />. Substituting this in gives us\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/3/3631c2c2ee1f2ddd5ac6bdea2abce7d889fb5590.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2R}{c} = \\frac{a}{\\frac{2\\Delta}{b}},\\]\" width=\"79\" height=\"44\" />\nwhich simplifies to\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/8/6f848d19d155fbdd99f5c597aaad7c1a03ba5978.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[R=\\frac{abc}{4\\Delta}\\text{ or }\\Delta=\\frac{abc}{4R}\\]\" width=\"159\" height=\"37\" />\nand we are done.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof_2\">Proof 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Proof 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>One can also prove this swiftly by knowledge of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Law_of_Sines\" title=\"Law of Sines\">extended law of sines</a>:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/0/030b2f38ce34806f1f86f4cca8b452120b4f15fc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2R = \\frac{a}{\\sin(\\angle A)}\\tag{1}\\]\" width=\"325\" height=\"37\" />\nSince the area, say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/7/0a737670d481129e2ead6d7223437f3cf70ea53f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, of the triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/c/19c5428c39f2bc890bdd36f37584ad267f0c31bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta = \\frac{1}{2}bc\\sin(\\angle A)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"136\" height=\"38\" />, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/d/bdd00730c37188a1e9cfb5cdd867cc014b5d4244.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{\\sin(\\angle A)} = \\frac{bc}{2\\Delta} \\Longrightarrow \\frac{a}{\\sin(\\angle A)} = \\frac{abc}{2\\Delta}\\tag{2}\\]\" width=\"406\" height=\"42\" />\nPlugging this back into (1) confirms\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/1/4315d2c13919430fc65b35800b49842ce989e606.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2R = \\frac{abc}{2\\Delta} \\Longrightarrow \\boxed{4R\\Delta = abc}\\]\" width=\"215\" height=\"37\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Formula_for_Circumradius\">Formula for Circumradius</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Formula for Circumradius\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/d/7/1d7cc49568f0bcc67ca49a02ce767e44fdfc235a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R =\t\\frac{abc}{4rs}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"66\" height=\"38\" />\nWhere <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is the circumradius, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is the inradius, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> are the respective sides of the triangle and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/2/ce27f4a313e96483f3afac3f780584283689353d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s = (a+b+c)/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"18\" /> is the semiperimeter. Note that this is similar to the previously mentioned formula; the reason being that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/6/6f6a8dfe6d1a7764e3d737e70a7baa2408be22e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A = rs$\" width=\"54\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>But, if you don't know the inradius, you can find the area of the triangle by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Heron%27s_Formula\" title=\"Heron&#39;s Formula\">Heron’s Formula</a>:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/a/90aae4e3782b58b13e74fd605e15bbb3428b27ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A=\\sqrt{s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"224\" height=\"32\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Circumradius.2C_bisector_and_altitude\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Circumradius,_bisector_and_altitude\">Circumradius, bisector and altitude</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Circumradius, bisector and altitude\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:R_H_L_angles.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/77/R_H_L_angles.png/400px-R_H_L_angles.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"389\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/7/77/R_H_L_angles.png/600px-R_H_L_angles.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/7/77/R_H_L_angles.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Circumradius and altitude are isogonals with respect bisector and vertex of triangle.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Euler.27s_Theorem_for_a_Triangle\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Euler's_Theorem_for_a_Triangle\">Euler's Theorem for a Triangle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Euler&#39;s Theorem for a Triangle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> have circumcenter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and incenter <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/0/d30d2cc82daeaaf90572fb9069456896a1310b78.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[OI^2=R(R-2r) \\implies R \\geq 2r\\]\" width=\"241\" height=\"19\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>See <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.cut-the-knot.org/triangle/EulerIO.shtml\">https://www.cut-the-knot.org/triangle/EulerIO.shtml</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Right_triangles\">Right triangles</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Right triangles\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The hypotenuse of the triangle is the diameter of its circumcircle, and the circumcenter is its midpoint, so the circumradius is equal to half of the hypotenuse of the right triangle.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/0/fd0444f5687fc5e166a7ee3efc071f7587b65c48.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pair A,B,C,I; A=(0,0); B=(0,3); C=(4,0); draw(A--B--C--cycle); I=circumcenter(A,B,C); draw(I--A,gray); label(&quot;$R$&quot;,(I+A)/2,NW,gray); draw(circumcircle(A,B,C)); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,I,N); dot(I); draw(rightanglemark(B,A,C,10)); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n</p><p>This results in a well-known theorem:\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Theorem\">Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>The midpoint of the hypotenuse is equidistant from the vertices of the right triangle.\nThe midpoint of the hypotenuse is the circumcenter of a right triangle.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Equilateral_triangles\">Equilateral triangles</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Equilateral triangles\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/c/93c2be874d6a6326362fe793c6327502d5c0de3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=\\frac{s}{\\sqrt3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"65\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/7/f37bba504894945c07a32f5496d74299a37aa51c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is the length of a side of the triangle.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/1/ca10ddc9a1840bd3a175658125dfc16d353a3b39.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pair A,B,C,I; A=(0,0); B=(1,0); C=intersectionpoint(arc(A,1,0,90),arc(B,1,90,180)); draw(A--B--C--cycle); I=circumcenter(A,B,C); draw(circumcircle(A,B,C)); label(&quot;$C$&quot;,I,E); dot(I); label(&quot;$s$&quot;,A--B,S); label(&quot;$s$&quot;,A--C,N); label(&quot;$s$&quot;,B--C,N); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"252\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"If_all_three_sides_are_known\">If all three sides are known</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: If all three sides are known\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/4/c/c4c7b9eb9022b22962e7eaa8f7357fcf855b5c95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=\\frac{abc}{\\sqrt{(a+b+c)(-a+b+c)(a-b+c)(a+b-c)}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"408\" height=\"45\" />\n</p><p><br />\nWhich follows from the Heron's Formula and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/d/bbde5d6a10f64624a7e76405e2ea632e4ecf1092.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R=\\frac{abc}{4A}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"65\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"If_you_know_just_one_side_and_its_opposite_angle\">If you know just one side and its opposite angle</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: If you know just one side and its opposite angle\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/d/fad5dd9b06bf7247f0a494cf9d31d357c94bbb2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2R=\\frac{a}{\\sin{A}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"88\" height=\"33\" /> by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Law_of_Sines\" title=\"Law of Sines\">Law of Sines</a>.\n</p><p>(Extended Law of Sines)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Circumradius&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Inradius\" title=\"Inradius\">Inradius</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Semiperimeter\" title=\"Semiperimeter\">Semiperimeter</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018022050\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.033 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.049 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 290/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 9621/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,ca